WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
 - So usually when we talk about a medical diagnosis,

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.440
 there's an expectation of precision.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.140
 - Right, it feels a lot like engineering.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.260
 - Exactly, like you break your arm,

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.040
 the X-ray shows that stark jagged white line

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.000
 right through the bone of the doctor just points to it.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.560
 - Yeah, it's broken or it's not broken.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.520
 - Right, it's a clean, comforting binary.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.020
 We love things to be visible and easily categorized.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:26.600
 But then you step into the world of neurology.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.160
 - And all that comfort just goes out the window.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.840
 - Oh, completely.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.280
 You cross the blood-brain barrier

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.200
 and simply that comforting X-ray machine

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.000
 is just utterly useless.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.960
 - Yeah, you're looking at a diagnostic landscape

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.040
 that is incredibly murky.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.200
 The signs are hidden and the consequences

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.880
 of misreading them are, well, they're instant.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.600
 - Which is exactly why today's deep dive is so critical.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.920
 We are decoding the ultimate control center

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.360
 of the human body for you.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.120
 - And our source material today is fantastic.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.000
 - It really is.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.680
 We're looking at a massive, highly detailed set

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.720
 of medical surgical nursing notes on neurology.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.920
 Specifically, Professor Marpol's week two breakdown

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:06.320
 of chapters 60 through 65.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.280
 - But, you know, we aren't just here to cram

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.360
 for a nursing exam.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.720
 - No, not at all.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.160
 Whether you're a medical professional standing

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.960
 at the bedside or just someone who is insanely curious

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.720
 about how those three pounds of electrified jelly

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.680
 inside your skull keep you alive, this is for you.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.280
 - We're talking about the frontline of life and death here,

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:25.120
 the survival mechanics of the brain

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.680
 from the outside in and the inside out.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.600
 - So, to navigate those mechanics,

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.520
 we really need to understand the fundamental triage philosophy

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.760
 that the notes establish right out of the gate.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.200
 - Right, because in neurology, doing the right thing

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.920
 at the wrong time isn't just a simple error.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:41.320
 - It can literally be fatal.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:42.360
 - Exactly.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.080
 So the framework provided is this four step method

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:46.840
 for prioritizing action.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.480
 It's grounded in a concept called AdPy.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:50.600
 - AdPy.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.520
 That's assess, diagnose, plan, implement, and evaluate, right?

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.360
 - You got it.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.400
 But the absolute critical modification for neuro is this.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.200
 If you do not have a complete clinical picture,

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.000
 you assess first.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.080
 - You assess first.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.880
 - Yeah, you never, ever act blindly on a neurological patient.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.720
 - Okay, I want to push back on that just slightly

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.840
 'cause it seems kind of contrary to what we're all taught

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.600
 in basic first aid.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:14.440
 - Also.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.200
 - Well, we've all had it drilled into our heads, right?

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.600
 ABCs, airway breathing circulation.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:19.440
 - Right, right.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.120
 - If someone collapses, you secure the airway immediately.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.480
 But the notes specifically weren't against picking

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.640
 the airway intervention by default.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.480
 Why would we delay securing an airway to do an assessment?

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.320
 - Well, because a neurological emergency

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.680
 often masquerades at something else entirely,

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.160
 or it presents multiple simultaneous crises.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.320
 - Okay, that makes sense.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.520
 - The rule is that an active problem

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.480
 always supersedes a potential risk.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.440
 - An active problem beats a risk.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.560
 - Exactly.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.560
 So imagine you're the ER nurse and a patient is brought in

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.760
 with a massive bleeding scalp laceration from the fall.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.240
 - Oh, wow, okay.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.240
 - They're awake, they're crying, yelling in pain.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.800
 The inexperienced instinct is to panic and think,

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.760
 "Head trauma, check the airway."

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.960
 - Right, because that's the ABCs kicking in.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.400
 - But if they are yelling at you,

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.280
 their airway is perfectly open.

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:08.120
 It's patent.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:09.080
 - Uh-huh, right.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:10.640
 You can't yell if you can't breathe.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.640
 - Exactly, so the active life-threatening problem

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.200
 right the second is the profound blood loss

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.360
 from that highly vascular scalp wound.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.680
 - You deal with the fire currently burning.

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:22.520
 - Yes.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:23.360
 - The hemorrhage.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.040
 - You deal with that before you worry about

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.160
 the theoretical risk of them losing their airway later.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.920
 - So it prevents cognitive tunnel vision.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.520
 You don't just blindly follow an acronym.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:37.120
 You evaluate the immediate physics of the situation.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.480
 - Perfectly said, and that leads to the fourth step

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.960
 of this triage method, which is choosing the safest,

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.680
 least invasive intervention first.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.960
 - Right, you don't drill a hole in the skull

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.120
 if just elevating the head of the bed

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.640
 fixes the pressure problem.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:56.400
 - Exactly, it is all about preserving homeostasis.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.680
 - Homeostasis, that really is the key

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.800
 to everything we are discussing today, isn't it?

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.840
 - It is, the nervous system does not operate on magic.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:07.240
 It operates on a desperate, relentless balancing act

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.600
 of fluid pressures, chemical gradients,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:10.960
 and electrical potentials.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.680
 - A constant steady state.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.440
 - Right, but when that balance tips,

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:19.040
 the body reveals a terrifying ruthless prioritization.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.280
 - Yeah, the notes highlight a grim reality here.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:23.600
 The brain dies for the outside in.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.120
 - From the outside in.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:27.640
 And from an evolutionary standpoint, it makes sense.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:30.080
 The cortex, the outer wrinkled layers

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.280
 is a massive energy drain.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:34.360
 - Which is wild because when we think of the brain,

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:35.640
 we think of the cortex.

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:36.800
 - Oh, for sure.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.960
 - Like the frontal lobe handling our complex decision-making,

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.760
 our personality, the parietal lobe mapping

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.200
 our physical space, the things that make you both you.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.800
 - But the body considers all of that

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.520
 to be an expendable luxury.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.760
 - Wow, expendable.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:54.320
 - Deep down at the base of the brain,

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.600
 right where it meets the spinal cord is the brainstem,

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.440
 the medulla, the pawns.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:04:59.520
 - The primitive core.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.640
 - Exactly.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:02.360
 It doesn't care about your memories,

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.920
 it doesn't care about your ability to speak

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.000
 or your capacity to do math.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:09.440
 The brainstem controls the absolute bare minimum

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:11.120
 for biological survival.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.840
 - Breathing and heart rate.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.600
 - Yes, the rhythm of your heart

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.400
 and the drive to inflate your lungs.

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.480
 - So if we look at this mechanically,

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:22.040
 is the brain basically functioning like a sinking battleship?

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.640
 - Oh, I like where this is going.

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.520
 - Okay, so imagine a mass of dreadnought

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.360
 takes a torpedo to the hull.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.720
 Water's flooding the upper decks.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:29.800
 - Right.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.240
 - The captain, which is the brainstem in this scenario,

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.800
 retreats to the deepest, most heavily armored bunker

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:35.960
 at the very bottom of the ship.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.240
 - Yes.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.000
 - And the captain's only objective

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.560
 is to keep the diesel engines running

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:41.840
 and the bilge pumps working

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:43.280
 until the absolute last second.

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:45.120
 - Right, whatever it takes.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.880
 - So the captain will seal the blast doors

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.160
 and happily sacrifice the grand ballroom,

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:51.640
 the radar array, the officer's quarters.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:54.240
 If it means the engine runs for one more hour.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:57.000
 - That is a phenomenal analogy.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.520
 The physiological ruthlessness of the human body

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.040
 mirrors that exact scenario.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.440
 - It's just so calculating.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:04.280
 - It really is.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.320
 The body will sacrifice the higher level traits

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:07.360
 to save the core.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:08.760
 We actually see this clinically

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.160
 in severe, late stage dementia.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:12.920
 - Oh really, how so?

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.920
 - Well, the outer layers of the cortex degrade first,

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:19.040
 the patient's personality fades, their memories vanish,

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:20.960
 and they lose the ability to speak

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.600
 or recognize faces long, long

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.080
 before their basic bodily functions fail.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.200
 - Because the brainstem is holding the line.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.120
 - Exactly, keeping the heart beating for years

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.440
 after the cortex is essentially gone.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.520
 - And we're gonna see this ruthlessness play out

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.280
 in the emergency room too.

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:39.640
 The notes dive into these permissive states,

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.120
 which we'll get into a bit later.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:43.120
 - Right, where we let things get bad

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:44.840
 to prevent them from getting worse.

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:46.160
 - Exactly.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:47.680
 Like the idea that a medical team

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:51.360
 will intentionally let a patient's blood pressure skyrocket

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.720
 to terrifying levels, risking blown blood vessels

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.040
 in the kidneys or the eyes just to force oxygenated blood

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:01.200
 down into that bunker to save the captain.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.960
 - But you know, to save that captain,

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:04.920
 we have to know what is actually happening

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:06.760
 inside the bunker.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:09.600
 - Which brings us to the core diagnostic dilemma.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.320
 - Right, the skull is an evolutionary marvel

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:14.800
 designed to protect the brain from blunt force trauma.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.720
 It is a closed, solid bone box.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.080
 - And we cannot see through it.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:21.320
 - We can't.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.440
 So when the ship is sinking, how do we find the leak?

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:26.560
 - We have to rely on imaging

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.280
 because we can't just take a standard X-ray, right?

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.080
 - No, because brain tissue and blood all look like

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.800
 a blurry gray cloud behind the bright white bone of the skull.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.240
 - So the absolute workhorse in a neuro emergency

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.240
 is the CT scan, computed tomography.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:43.680
 - Yeah, a CT scan is essentially a three-dimensional X-ray.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.160
 It spins around the head, calculating the density

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:47.720
 of the tissues it passes through.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:48.640
 - And it's fast.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:50.760
 - Incredibly fast, which is its primary advantage

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.920
 in an emergency, it takes minutes.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:54.320
 - And because it measures density,

00:07:54.320 --> 00:07:56.640
 it is exceptionally good at finding fresh,

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:57.480
 clotted blood, right?

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.880
 - Yes, acute hemorrhage is very dense.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.000
 So it shows up as a bright, hyper-dense white mass

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.040
 on the scan against the darker gray

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:06.480
 of the normal brain tissue.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.440
 - But the notes highlight a massive blind spot

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:11.720
 with CT scans that I think most people just don't realize.

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:12.800
 - The thick slices?

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:16.560
 - Yes, the machine takes cross-sectional images slices

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.040
 of the brain, but in a standard rapid emergency CT,

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:21.640
 it uses thick slices.

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:23.960
 - Right, there are literal physical gaps

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:25.640
 between the images and captures.

00:08:25.640 --> 00:08:28.960
 - So if the scanner is taking a slice every five millimeters,

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.880
 but the patient has a tiny three millimeter microannurism

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:33.080
 that just ruptured.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:35.480
 That bleed can literally hide in the physical gap

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:37.000
 between slice two and slice three.

00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.120
 - That's terrifying.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:38.960
 - It is.

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:42.440
 A physician might look at that CT scan and say it's clear,

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:44.440
 but a negative CT scan does not mean

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:46.160
 the brain is perfectly fine.

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:47.760
 It just means there is no massive

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.880
 catastrophic structural abnormality.

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:52.680
 - And to make those structures more visible,

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.560
 they often inject an intravenous contrast eye,

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:56.840
 usually iodine-based?

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.360
 - Right, to light up the blood vessels.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.040
 - But this is where the nursing role becomes so paramount.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:03.560
 The machine does the imaging,

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:06.720
 but the nurse manages the physiological fallout.

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.720
 - Because contrast eye is highly nephrotoxic.

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.080
 - Meaning it's incredibly hard on the kidneys.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.560
 - Extremely, it's a thick viscous substance

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:18.240
 that the renal system struggles to filter out.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:20.280
 - So as a nurse, you aren't diagnosing

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:22.400
 the pixel density on the monitor.

00:09:22.400 --> 00:09:24.240
 - No, your job is to keep the patient safe

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:25.840
 during the aftermath.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.080
 - How do you do that?

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:29.080
 - Well, if the patient is alert and going home,

00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:31.640
 you instruct them to drink massive amounts of water.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:33.400
 - And if they're Nakoma?

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:34.680
 - Then you are hanging leaders

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:36.600
 of normal saline IV fluids.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:38.240
 You have to create hydraulic pressure

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.280
 in the vascular system to physically flush

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:42.080
 that heavy iodine dye through the kidneys

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:43.440
 before it crystallizes.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.920
 - Before it causes acute renal failure.

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:47.040
 - Exactly.

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:47.880
 - Wow.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.640
 - We also have PET scans, which are fascinating

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.160
 because they don't look at structure,

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:52.240
 they look at metabolism.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:54.720
 - Right, they measure how the brain is using glucose.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:56.160
 - But they require injecting

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:57.800
 or inhaling radioactive tracers.

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.200
 So there is a strict contraindication

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:01.760
 for pregnant women and children,

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.280
 unless it's a literal life or death necessity.

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:05.200
 - Right.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:07.600
 But if we want the absolute gold standard

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:10.320
 for structural detail, we skip the radiation entirely

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:11.400
 and move to the MRI.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:13.120
 - Magnetic resonance imaging.

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:14.080
 - Yes.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.840
 Unlike a CT scan, which relies on ionizing radiation,

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:22.000
 an MRI uses a giant superconducting magnet and radio waves

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.400
 to align the protons in the water molecules of your body.

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:28.520
 - It creates just breathtaking crystal clear,

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:30.680
 incredibly thin slices of tissue.

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:33.040
 - It can spot the tiniest lesions, tumors,

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:34.920
 or areas of demyelination.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.480
 - But the trade-off is brutal.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.640
 - It takes 30 to 60 minutes.

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:39.680
 - Yeah, and the patient is slid

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:41.320
 into a tiny claustrophobic tube

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:42.720
 that sounds like a jackhammer.

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:45.720
 - If the patient is confused, agitated, or claustrophobic,

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:48.080
 they simply cannot hold still long enough.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.960
 The images will be completely blurry.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:52.400
 - Which requires anticipation from the staff.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:53.240
 - Absolutely.

00:10:53.240 --> 00:10:56.000
 A skilled neuro nurse recognizes an agitated patient

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:59.480
 and advocates for a sedative or anti-anxiety medication

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:01.520
 before transport to radiology.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:05.080
 - But the true danger of an MRI isn't the claustrophobia, is it?

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:08.000
 - No, it is the physics of the machine itself.

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:08.840
 - The magnet.

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:11.040
 - The magnetic field is immensely powerful,

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:15.520
 usually 1.5 to 3 Tesla, which is tens of thousands

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:17.720
 of times stronger than the Earth's magnetic field.

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:19.320
 - And it is always on.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:20.160
 - Always.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.840
 You don't just flip a switch to turn off an MRI.

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:25.960
 - So any ferromagnetic metal brought into that room,

00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:28.880
 scissors, an oxygen tank, a hairpin,

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:31.040
 instantly becomes a lethal projectile.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:33.120
 - Accelerating at terrifying speeds

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:34.480
 toward the center of the tube.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:37.120
 - This creates a critical safety checkpoint.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:39.000
 - You have to screen for internal hardware.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:42.160
 - Old pacemaker is certain aneurysm clips, shrapnel.

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:44.840
 - Because if a pacemaker is pulled by that magnet?

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:47.880
 - It can literally be torn from the chest wall.

00:11:47.880 --> 00:11:50.440
 Or the electronics will just fry instantly.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:54.520
 - The notes have a very specific, almost cynical warning here.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.080
 Never trust a patient who says a piercing is titanium.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:11:59.880
 - I love that rule.

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:03.080
 Pure titanium is non-ferrous, meaning it isn't magnetic,

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:06.720
 but body jewelry is notoriously unregulated.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:09.080
 - So if a patient has a dermal piercing

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:12.080
 and they assure you it's surgical grade titanium,

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:13.200
 you just can't take that risk.

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:14.400
 - You cannot.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:18.040
 If that metal is actually an alloy containing iron or nickel,

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:20.040
 the moment they enter the magnetic field,

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:22.560
 the MRI will violently rip the jewelry

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:24.400
 straight out of their skin and tissue.

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:25.600
 - Oh, that's horrific.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:27.920
 - You have to tape it, remove it,

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:29.960
 or use a strong hand magnet to test it

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.200
 before they ever cross the threshold of the MRI suite.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.240
 - Okay, so that covers looking through the skull.

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:38.280
 But sometimes we need to actually analyze

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:39.680
 the fluid inside it.

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:42.680
 - Right, the cerebrospinal fluid or CSF.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:44.480
 - This is the clear fluid that bays the brain

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:47.520
 and spinal cord, bringing nutrients and removing waste.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:49.240
 And to get it, we do a lumbar puncture,

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:50.760
 often called a spinal tap.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:52.040
 - Exactly.

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:53.440
 The brain and spinal cord are wrapped

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:56.320
 in a protective membrane called the dura motor.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:00.160
 To access the CSF, a physician inserts a long hollow needle

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:02.160
 between the vertebrae and the lower back,

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:05.360
 piercing the dura and drawing out a few vials of fluid.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:07.440
 - And they test that for meningitis,

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:11.400
 multiple sclerosis markers, or subarachnoid hemorrhage.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:12.240
 - Yes.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:14.040
 - But the mechanics of getting the needle in

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:16.680
 require very specific positioning.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:19.920
 - The patient has to curl into a tight fetal position

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:20.960
 on their side.

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:24.080
 Or sit up and lean heavily over a tray table,

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:25.880
 arching their back like an angry cat.

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:26.800
 - Right.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:28.960
 You're physically trying to open up the spaces

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:31.680
 between the spinous processes of the vertebrae,

00:13:31.680 --> 00:13:34.160
 so the needle can slide through without hitting bone.

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:36.480
 - But the real physiological drama happens

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:37.880
 after the needle is removed.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:39.640
 - It really does.

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:43.480
 - When you puncture the dura, you create a microscopic hole.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:45.240
 - Usually it seals itself.

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:48.040
 - But sometimes it doesn't.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:49.960
 - And the CSF, which is under pressure,

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:52.240
 continues to leak out internally

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:54.480
 into the surrounding epidural space.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:55.840
 - I want to trace the mechanics of this

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:58.200
 because it perfectly sets up our next major topic.

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.200
 - Okay, let's do it.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:01.760
 - The brain weighs about three pounds,

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:05.560
 but it floats in a bath of about 150 milliliters of CSF.

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:06.440
 - Right.

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:08.920
 - That buoyancy makes the brain effectively weigh

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:11.160
 only a couple of ounces inside the skull.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:12.200
 - Yes.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:14.880
 - So if the CSF leaks out of that puncture hole

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:19.240
 in the lower back, the fluid volume drops system wide.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:22.640
 - Exactly, the brain loses its buoyant cushion.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:23.960
 Gravity takes over.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:27.120
 - The entire three pound mass of the brain

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:29.080
 literally sags downward.

00:14:29.080 --> 00:14:31.760
 - As it sags, it pulls on the meninges,

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:33.880
 the sensitive tissues wrapping the brain

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:35.480
 and anchoring it to the skull.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:37.480
 - And the meninges are highly innervated

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:38.720
 with pain receptors.

00:14:38.720 --> 00:14:42.280
 - So when the brain sags and stretches those anchors,

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:45.720
 the patient experiences a severe blinding headache.

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:48.440
 - Known as a post-lumbar puncture headache,

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:49.720
 or a spinal headache.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:50.600
 - Yes.

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:52.000
 - And the defining characteristic

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:54.040
 is that it is strictly positional.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:54.880
 - Right.

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:57.520
 If they lie down, gravity stops pulling the brain down,

00:14:57.520 --> 00:14:59.920
 the tension releases and the pain improves.

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:01.600
 - But the second they sit or stand up,

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:03.560
 the brain drops, the anchors pull tight

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:05.560
 and the agony returns.

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:07.880
 - Which dictates the nursing intervention.

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:10.440
 It is brilliantly simple physics.

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:12.520
 - First, you keep them perfectly flat

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:14.440
 for several hours post-procedure

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:16.040
 to prevent gravity from pulling fluid

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:17.360
 out of the puncture site.

00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:18.880
 - And to keep the brain supported.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:21.760
 - Second, you aggressively rehydrate them.

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:24.160
 - Pushing IV fluids in oral water.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:26.560
 - You are trying to increase their overall fluid volume

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:29.400
 to stimulate the rapid production of more CSF,

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.600
 refilling the tank and floating the brain back up

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:33.200
 to its proper position.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:35.720
 - That dynamic, the delicate balance of fluid

00:15:35.720 --> 00:15:37.640
 and pressure inside the skull

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:40.320
 is the absolute crux of neurology.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:41.600
 - It really is.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:43.800
 - We just saw what happens when fluid leaks out

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:46.720
 and pressure drops, the brain sags.

00:15:46.720 --> 00:15:50.320
 But what happens when the fluid or blood or swelling

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:53.800
 builds up inside that impenetrable black box

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:55.360
 and the pressure rises?

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:57.320
 - That brings us to one of the most critical concepts

00:15:57.320 --> 00:15:59.320
 in all of critical care medicine.

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:02.040
 - Intracranial pressure or ICP.

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:03.800
 - And the Monroe-Kelly doctrine.

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:05.400
 - The Monroe-Kelly doctrine.

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:07.760
 This is the fundamental law of the skull.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:09.960
 - The adult skull is rigid bone.

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:12.040
 The fontanel is the soft spots we have as babies

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:13.440
 are fused solid.

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:14.960
 - The box cannot expand.

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:15.800
 - Exactly.

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:18.000
 - Inside this fixed, unyielding box,

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:19.800
 you have exactly three components.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:22.840
 - Brain tissue, which takes up about 80% of the space.

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:25.640
 - Blood volume within the vessels takes up about 10%.

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:29.000
 - And the cerebrospinal fluid takes up the remaining 10%.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:31.360
 - The doctrine states a simple truth of physics

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:34.560
 because the total volume of the skull cannot change

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:37.600
 if any one of those three components increases in volume.

00:16:37.600 --> 00:16:40.640
 - One or both of the other two must decrease to make room.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:41.800
 - They have to.

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:43.840
 If they don't, the pressure inside the skull

00:16:43.840 --> 00:16:45.560
 will rise exponentially.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:48.240
 - I picture this like a popular nightclub,

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:51.640
 operating at absolute maximum fire code capacity.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:52.960
 - Okay, I like this.

00:16:52.960 --> 00:16:54.960
 - The walls of the club are solid steel.

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:56.640
 They cannot bend or stretch.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:57.480
 - Right.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:16:59.880
 - Inside, you have 80 regular patrons

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:01.800
 representing the brain tissue.

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:04.560
 You have 10 bartenders representing the blood

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:07.560
 and you have 10 bouncers representing the CSF.

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:09.280
 - The club is completely full.

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:10.240
 - Yes.

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:13.680
 Now, let's say a massive fight breaks out outside

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:16.720
 and 10 more regular patrons rush into the club to hide.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:21.160
 - That's a tumor growing or brain tissue swelling.

00:17:21.160 --> 00:17:22.680
 - According to the doctrine,

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:25.360
 to prevent the steel walls from rupturing,

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:27.520
 the club must eject someone.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.120
 - It will try to kick out 10 bouncers.

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:33.000
 The body will shunt CSF down into the spinal column

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.280
 to make room.

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:35.960
 - And if that isn't enough,

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:38.720
 - It will start kicking out the bartenders.

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:40.880
 The body will compress the veins in the brain

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:42.720
 to squeeze blood out.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:44.600
 - But what happens when there are no more bouncers

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:46.200
 or bartenders to kick out?

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:48.040
 - The tumor keeps growing.

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:50.240
 The swelling keeps expanding.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.360
 - The pressure inside the club builds and builds,

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:55.280
 but the steel walls won't break.

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:58.080
 - So the pressure looks for the only exit available.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:01.560
 - And in the human skull, there is only one exit.

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:03.120
 - A hole at the very base of the skull

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.080
 called the foramen magnum.

00:18:05.080 --> 00:18:07.880
 - Where the spinal cord attaches to the brainstem.

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:09.160
 - Exactly.

00:18:09.160 --> 00:18:12.400
 When the pressure exhausts all compensatory mechanisms,

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:15.200
 the brain tissue is violently squeezed downward.

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:16.440
 - Extruding through that hole

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.400
 like tooth base being crushed out of a tube.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:20.640
 - This is called herniation.

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:23.080
 - And what sits right above the foramen magnum?

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:24.160
 - Our captain.

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:25.320
 - The brainstem.

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:26.400
 - Exactly.

00:18:26.400 --> 00:18:29.160
 - The herniating brain tissue crashes directly

00:18:29.160 --> 00:18:31.360
 into the brainstem, crushing the centers

00:18:31.360 --> 00:18:33.880
 that control breathing and heart rate.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:37.200
 - Herniation is a fatal irreversible event.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:39.480
 - Brain death is instantaneous.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:41.440
 So measuring and managing this pressure

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:44.320
 is literally the difference between life and death.

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:46.720
 - Normal intracranial pressure is strictly defined

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:49.880
 as between 10 and 20 millimeters of mercury.

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:52.160
 - Anything above 20 is a crisis.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:54.360
 - But the notes make a crucial point.

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.080
 ICP doesn't exist in a vacuum.

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:57.760
 It is in a constant battle

00:18:57.760 --> 00:18:59.960
 with the blood trying to pump into the head.

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:01.000
 - Right.

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:03.280
 The pressure of the blood pushing into the brain

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:06.240
 is your mean arterial pressure or map.

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:08.480
 - It's an average of your overall blood pressure.

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:09.320
 - And the relationship

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:11.440
 between the blood pushing and the map

00:19:11.440 --> 00:19:14.480
 and the swelling pushing back out, the ICP,

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:17.600
 gives us the most important equation in neurocritical care.

00:19:17.600 --> 00:19:20.960
 - Cerebral perfusion pressure or CPP.

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:23.520
 - Perfusion means the actual physical delivery

00:19:23.520 --> 00:19:26.640
 of oxygenated blood into the microscopic capillary beds

00:19:26.640 --> 00:19:27.600
 of the brain tissue.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:28.440
 - Right.

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:29.800
 And the equation is simple.

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:33.440
 Map minus ICP equals CPP.

00:19:33.440 --> 00:19:34.480
 - The force of the pump

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:36.240
 minus the resistance of the box.

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:37.120
 - Exactly.

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:41.320
 - Normal CPP is between 70 and 100 millimeters of mercury.

00:19:41.320 --> 00:19:43.400
 - If the CPP drops below 50,

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:46.040
 the brain tissue is actively starving for oxygen

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:49.120
 and irreversible is schematic damage begins.

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:51.160
 - The absolute floor for human survival,

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:54.960
 the magic number you must fight for, is a CPP of 60.

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:56.760
 - Let's put you, the listener,

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:58.880
 in the shoes of an ICU nurse

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:01.560
 and walk through the math of a dying brain

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:04.200
 because this is where the theory becomes terrifyingly real.

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:05.520
 - Let's do it.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:07.600
 - Imagine you were standing at the bedside.

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:10.640
 Your patient is a young woman who's in a car crash.

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:12.600
 She has traumatic brain swelling.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:13.440
 - Okay.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:14.520
 - You look at the monitor,

00:20:14.520 --> 00:20:18.520
 the doctors have set a goal for her map to be 65,

00:20:18.520 --> 00:20:20.280
 which is a very standard, healthy target

00:20:20.280 --> 00:20:21.720
 in an intensive care unit.

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:22.840
 - Very normal.

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:25.480
 - Her map is exactly 65.

00:20:25.480 --> 00:20:28.280
 Her blood pressure looks perfectly stable.

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:31.280
 Now you look at her intracranial pressure monitor.

00:20:31.280 --> 00:20:32.920
 Her ICP is 18.

00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:35.640
 - Let's analyze that.

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:38.560
 - An ICP of 18 is technically normal, correct?

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:40.880
 It is under the danger threshold of 20.

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:42.480
 - Right, it's under 20.

00:20:42.480 --> 00:20:45.840
 So a casual observer might think her blood pressure is fine,

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:47.800
 her brain pressure is normal.

00:20:47.800 --> 00:20:48.720
 She's doing great.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:50.600
 - But let's do the math.

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:54.760
 - Map is 65, minus the ICP of 18.

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:59.520
 That leaves a cerebral perfusion pressure of 47.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:02.840
 - 47 is below the survival threshold of 50.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:06.160
 Even though both individual numbers, the map and the ICP

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:08.160
 are technically within acceptable limits.

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:11.040
 - The relationship between them is lethal.

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:14.160
 - The pressure inside the skull, while not quite 20,

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:17.520
 is still too strong for a map of 65 to overcome.

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:20.160
 - The heart is trying to push blood up into the skull,

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:22.280
 but the resistance is just too high.

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:24.320
 The blood simply cannot perfuse the tissue.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:26.760
 - The patient's brain is literally suffocating

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:29.960
 while the monitor shows normal vitals.

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:32.480
 - This is exactly why you have to treat the patient

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:34.320
 and the entire clinical picture,

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:36.440
 not just a single green number on a screen.

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:40.520
 - To fix the CPP of 47, you only have two mathematical options.

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:44.560
 - You either have to lower the ICP or you have to raise the map.

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:46.240
 - And this brings us back to the concept

00:21:46.240 --> 00:21:48.520
 of permissive states we mentioned earlier.

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:52.200
 - Right, if you cannot get the brain swelling down fast enough,

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:54.200
 you have to raise the blood pressure.

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:56.920
 - You will deliberately administer massive doses

00:21:56.920 --> 00:21:59.720
 of vasopressor medications like norepinephrine

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:02.440
 to intentionally skyrocket the patient's blood pressure.

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:04.720
 - You are using brute hydraulic force

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:07.120
 to batter down the resistance of the swollen brain

00:22:07.120 --> 00:22:09.600
 and force oxygen into the tissue.

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:12.360
 - But outside of an ICU with a probe drilled directly

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:14.280
 into the skull, you don't have a screen

