00:51:45.400 --> 00:51:47.080
 which controls the diaphragm.

00:51:47.080 --> 00:51:49.800
 - The patient instantly loses the ability to breathe

00:51:49.800 --> 00:51:52.440
 and requires permanent mechanical ventilation.

00:51:52.440 --> 00:51:55.160
 - But surviving the initial horrific trauma

00:51:55.160 --> 00:51:58.160
 of a spinal cord injury isn't the end of the danger.

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:00.280
 - Years or decades later,

00:52:00.280 --> 00:52:03.160
 patients with injuries in their mid back or higher

00:52:03.160 --> 00:52:06.440
 live with a ticking time bomb inside their own nervous system.

00:52:06.440 --> 00:52:09.360
 - It's a condition called autonomic dysreflexia.

00:52:09.360 --> 00:52:12.200
 - Autonomic dysreflexia is perhaps one of the most

00:52:12.200 --> 00:52:15.360
 fascinating and terrifying physiological chain reactions

00:52:15.360 --> 00:52:16.880
 in all of human biology.

00:52:16.880 --> 00:52:19.880
 - It occurs exclusively in patients with spinal cord injuries

00:52:19.880 --> 00:52:23.560
 at or above the level of the sixth thoracic vertebra, T6,

00:52:23.560 --> 00:52:25.440
 which is roughly the mid chest level.

00:52:25.440 --> 00:52:27.440
 - Let's spend some time tracing the nerve signals here

00:52:27.440 --> 00:52:31.360
 because it is literally a civil war inside the body

00:52:31.360 --> 00:52:33.840
 and understanding the mechanics is mind-blowing.

00:52:33.840 --> 00:52:36.480
 - It almost always starts with something completely mundane

00:52:36.480 --> 00:52:39.600
 happening in the lower half of the body, a full bladder.

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:41.320
 - Let's say the patient's fully catheter

00:52:41.320 --> 00:52:44.240
 that gets kinked under their leg in a wheelchair.

00:52:44.240 --> 00:52:46.080
 The bladder continues to fill with urine,

00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:48.680
 stretching to maximum capacity.

00:52:48.680 --> 00:52:51.360
 - Step one, the profoundly stretched bladder wall

00:52:51.360 --> 00:52:53.960
 generates massive sensory pain signals.

00:52:53.960 --> 00:52:56.000
 - Those signals travel along sensory nerves

00:52:56.000 --> 00:52:57.640
 to the base of the spinal cord

00:52:57.640 --> 00:52:59.680
 and they begin racing upward toward the brain

00:52:59.680 --> 00:53:02.120
 to deliver the message, we are in agony,

00:53:02.120 --> 00:53:03.800
 we need to empty the bladder.

00:53:03.800 --> 00:53:07.360
 - Step two, that sensory signal races up the spinal cord,

00:53:07.360 --> 00:53:10.040
 but it hits a literal brick wall.

00:53:10.040 --> 00:53:12.960
 - The side of the old spinal cord injury at T6.

00:53:12.960 --> 00:53:14.960
 - The scar tissue physically prevents the signal

00:53:14.960 --> 00:53:16.320
 from reaching the brain.

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:18.840
 The brain has no idea the bladder is full.

00:53:18.840 --> 00:53:21.040
 - The patient feels absolutely no pain,

00:53:21.040 --> 00:53:24.600
 but the signal doesn't just evaporate, it rebounds.

00:53:24.600 --> 00:53:26.960
 - Step three, the blocked distress signal

00:53:26.960 --> 00:53:29.600
 triggers a massive localized reflex action

00:53:29.600 --> 00:53:32.840
 in the sympathetic nervous system, the fight or flight system,

00:53:32.840 --> 00:53:35.920
 in the spinal cord below the level of the injury.

00:53:35.920 --> 00:53:37.400
 - Without the brain there to regulate it,

00:53:37.400 --> 00:53:39.880
 the sympathetic nervous system goes completely eroke.

00:53:39.880 --> 00:53:42.560
 It floods the lower half of the body with norepinephrine,

00:53:42.560 --> 00:53:45.400
 causing profound violent vasoconstriction.

00:53:45.400 --> 00:53:47.320
 All the blood vessels in the legs and abdomen

00:53:47.320 --> 00:53:49.080
 clamp down hard.

00:53:49.080 --> 00:53:52.120
 - Step four, because all those massive blood vessels

00:53:52.120 --> 00:53:54.520
 in the lower half of the body just clamped shut,

00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:56.480
 the blood is violently squeezed upward

00:53:56.480 --> 00:53:58.320
 into the top half of the body.

00:53:58.320 --> 00:54:01.240
 - The systemic blood pressure, absolutely skyrockets.

00:54:01.240 --> 00:54:05.240
 We are talking lethal hemorrhagic stroke level hypertension,

00:54:05.240 --> 00:54:09.080
 a blood pressure of 250 over 150 in seconds.

00:54:09.080 --> 00:54:13.040
 - Step five, the brain, sitting safely up above the injury,

00:54:13.040 --> 00:54:16.240
 suddenly detects this massive spike in blood pressure.

00:54:16.240 --> 00:54:19.280
 - The baroreceptors in the next screen, pressure is critical.

00:54:19.280 --> 00:54:22.720
 We are going to blow an aneurysm, the brain panics.

00:54:22.720 --> 00:54:25.440
 - Step six, the brain launches a massive counterattack

00:54:25.440 --> 00:54:26.840
 to lower the blood pressure.

00:54:26.840 --> 00:54:29.160
 It activates the parasympathetic nervous system,

00:54:29.160 --> 00:54:31.160
 the rest and digest side.

00:54:31.160 --> 00:54:33.160
 - First it uses the vagus nerve,

00:54:33.160 --> 00:54:35.640
 because the vagus nerve runs outside the spinal cord

00:54:35.640 --> 00:54:38.160
 directly from the brain stem to the heart.

00:54:38.160 --> 00:54:40.040
 Its signal gets through perfectly.

00:54:40.040 --> 00:54:42.160
 - The brain orders the heart to slow down

00:54:42.160 --> 00:54:43.600
 to drop the pressure.

00:54:43.600 --> 00:54:46.160
