00:56:23.360 --> 00:56:25.040
 - You unkink the tube.

00:56:25.040 --> 00:56:26.400
 The bladder drains.

00:56:26.400 --> 00:56:28.280
 The sensory signal stops.

00:56:28.280 --> 00:56:30.840
 The sympathetic nervous system calms down

00:56:30.840 --> 00:56:34.080
 and the blood pressure normalizes in minutes.

00:56:34.080 --> 00:56:35.120
 - If it isn't the bladder,

00:56:35.120 --> 00:56:37.040
 you check for a bowel impaction.

00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:39.800
 Sometimes the trigger is as small as a wrinkled bed sheet

00:56:39.800 --> 00:56:42.840
 pressing hard into their skin or an ingrown toenail.

00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:45.920
 You must remove the painful stimulus.

00:56:45.920 --> 00:56:48.440
 - Trauma physically severs the nurse,

00:56:48.440 --> 00:56:50.360
 which is brutal and mechanical.

00:56:50.360 --> 00:56:52.760
 But sometimes the body's own defense system

00:56:52.760 --> 00:56:55.520
 does the severing, it attacks itself.

00:56:55.520 --> 00:56:57.120
 - This brings us to infections

00:56:57.120 --> 00:57:00.360
 and the bizarre world of autoimmune mysteries.

00:57:00.360 --> 00:57:02.760
 - Let's look at meningitis first.

00:57:02.760 --> 00:57:04.560
 We mentioned the meninges earlier,

00:57:04.560 --> 00:57:05.640
 the protective membranes

00:57:05.640 --> 00:57:07.760
 wrapping the brain and spinal cord.

00:57:07.760 --> 00:57:09.320
 - Meningitis is an infection

00:57:09.320 --> 00:57:12.200
 and severe inflammation of those membranes.

00:57:12.200 --> 00:57:14.600
 Bacterial meningitis is incredibly dangerous

00:57:14.600 --> 00:57:16.120
 and highly contagious,

00:57:16.120 --> 00:57:18.560
 spreading rapidly through respiratory droplets

00:57:18.560 --> 00:57:21.160
 and crowded environments like military barracks

00:57:21.160 --> 00:57:22.840
 or college dormitories.

00:57:22.840 --> 00:57:25.320
 - The signs are classic and aggressive.

00:57:25.320 --> 00:57:27.400
 - A high fever, photophobia,

00:57:27.400 --> 00:57:30.360
 which is a severe painful sensitivity to light,

00:57:30.360 --> 00:57:32.240
 and an incredibly stiff neck,

00:57:32.240 --> 00:57:34.320
 known as nucal rigidity.

00:57:34.320 --> 00:57:36.520
 The patient physically cannot touch their chin

00:57:36.520 --> 00:57:37.760
 to their chest.

00:57:37.760 --> 00:57:40.640
 Furthermore, the inflammation causes extra fluid production,

00:57:40.640 --> 00:57:43.840
 which raises our old enemy intracranial pressure,

00:57:43.840 --> 00:57:46.240
 leading to an agonizing headache.

00:57:46.240 --> 00:57:48.360
 - The notes detail two physical tests for meningitis

00:57:48.360 --> 00:57:50.120
 that rely entirely on the mechanics

00:57:50.120 --> 00:57:52.040
 of the inflamed spinal cord.

00:57:52.040 --> 00:57:53.640
 The first is kerning sign.

00:57:53.640 --> 00:57:55.120
 - You lie the patient flat,

00:57:55.120 --> 00:57:57.480
 bend their hip and knee to 90 degrees,

00:57:57.480 --> 00:57:59.240
 and then try to straighten their leg.

00:57:59.240 --> 00:58:02.160
 It causes severe pain in the lower back and resistance.

00:58:02.160 --> 00:58:05.520
 - The more reliable test is Brudzinski sign.

00:58:05.520 --> 00:58:06.880
 If you lie the patient flat

00:58:06.880 --> 00:58:09.320
 and physically bend their neck forward,

00:58:09.320 --> 00:58:11.280
 pulling their chin toward their chest,

00:58:11.280 --> 00:58:14.560
 their hips and knees will reflexively pull upward.

00:58:14.560 --> 00:58:17.360
 - Why does bending the neck make the knees bend?

00:58:17.360 --> 00:58:19.320
 - It's a mechanical tethering issue.

00:58:19.320 --> 00:58:22.000
 The meningies wrap all the way down the spinal cord.

00:58:22.000 --> 00:58:24.680
 When they're infected, they become swollen, rigid,

00:58:24.680 --> 00:58:27.440
 and exquisitely sensitive to stretching.

00:58:27.440 --> 00:58:28.600
 - Bending the neck forward

00:58:28.600 --> 00:58:30.600
 pulls the entire spinal cord upward,

00:58:30.600 --> 00:58:32.920
 stretching those inflamed meninges.

00:58:32.920 --> 00:58:34.880
 The body reflexively bends the knees and hips

00:58:34.880 --> 00:58:36.960
 to introduce slack into the spinal nerve roots,

00:58:36.960 --> 00:58:39.240
 relieving the agonizing tension on the cord.

00:58:39.240 --> 00:58:41.160
 - The treatment for bacterial meningitis

00:58:41.160 --> 00:58:43.200
 requires massive IV antibiotics

00:58:43.200 --> 00:58:45.960
 that are capable of crossing the blood brain barrier.

00:58:45.960 --> 00:58:47.960
 - But the notes highlight a very specific

00:58:47.960 --> 00:58:50.680
 critical sequence of medication administration.

00:58:50.680 --> 00:58:53.560
 You must give a potent steroid, dexamethasone,

00:58:53.560 --> 00:58:55.840
 before or at the exact same time

00:58:55.840 --> 00:58:57.960
 as the first dose of antibiotics.

00:58:57.960 --> 00:58:59.520
 - Why would we suppress the immune system

00:58:59.520 --> 00:59:02.480
 with a steroid when we are fighting a massive infection?

00:59:02.480 --> 00:59:04.440
 - Because of how the antibiotics work.

00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:06.280
 When the antibiotic hits the bacteria,

00:59:06.280 --> 00:59:08.040
 it doesn't just neatly poison them.

00:59:08.040 --> 00:59:10.280
 It causes bacterial lysis.

00:59:10.280 --> 00:59:12.360
 - The bacteria violently burst open,

00:59:12.360 --> 00:59:14.440
 spilling all their toxic internal contents

00:59:14.440 --> 00:59:18.640
 and endotoxins directly into the cerebral spinal fluid.

00:59:18.640 --> 00:59:21.440
 - The immune system detects this massive dump of toxins

00:59:21.440 --> 00:59:23.240
 and launches a cytokine storm,

00:59:23.240 --> 00:59:25.680
 a catastrophic inflammatory response.

00:59:25.680 --> 00:59:27.520
 The meningies swell so rapidly

00:59:27.520 --> 00:59:30.080
 that the brain herniates and the patient dies.

00:59:30.080 --> 00:59:32.200
 - By giving the dexamethasone first,

00:59:32.200 --> 00:59:34.800
 you preemptively blunt the immune system's inflammatory

00:59:34.800 --> 00:59:36.800
 response, preventing the fatal swelling

00:59:36.800 --> 00:59:38.720
 when the bacteria explode.

00:59:38.720 --> 00:59:41.880
 - That is incredible pharmacological choreography.

00:59:41.880 --> 00:59:43.440
 While infections attack the nervous system

00:59:43.440 --> 00:59:45.880
 from the outside, auto immune diseases

00:59:45.880 --> 00:59:47.560
 are a betrayal from within.

00:59:47.560 --> 00:59:50.360
 - The immune system suddenly misidentifies normal,

00:59:50.360 --> 00:59:53.800
 healthy parts of the nervous system as foreign invaders

00:59:53.800 --> 00:59:56.120
 and systematically destroys them.

00:59:56.120 --> 00:59:58.760
 - The notes cover three major auto immune conditions,

00:59:58.760 --> 01:00:00.680
 each targeting a different mechanism.

01:00:00.680 --> 01:00:04.200
 Let's start with multiple sclerosis or MS.

01:00:04.200 --> 01:00:06.120
 - MS is a demyelinating disease

01:00:06.120 --> 01:00:07.520
 at the central nervous system,

01:00:07.520 --> 01:00:09.680
 the brain and the spinal cord.

01:00:09.680 --> 01:00:11.400
 - I find the easiest way to picture MS

01:00:11.400 --> 01:00:14.480
 is to look at the electrical wiring in a house.

01:00:14.480 --> 01:00:18.520
 The actual nerve fibers, the axons are the copper wires.

01:00:18.520 --> 01:00:20.760
 - But copper wires need to be insulated

01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:22.760
 or the electricity leaks out.

01:00:22.760 --> 01:00:25.320
 In the body, that insulation is a fatty substance

01:00:25.320 --> 01:00:26.680
 called myelin.

01:00:26.680 --> 01:00:29.520
 - The myelin sheath wraps around the axon and segments,

01:00:29.520 --> 01:00:31.840
 allowing the electrical signal to jump rapidly

01:00:31.840 --> 01:00:36.000
 from gap to gap, a process called saltatory conduction.

01:00:36.000 --> 01:00:37.600
 - It is what makes human reflexes

01:00:37.600 --> 01:00:40.280
 and thoughts so lightning fast.

01:00:40.280 --> 01:00:41.920
 In multiple sclerosis,

01:00:41.920 --> 01:00:43.960
 the immune system distaches T-cells

01:00:43.960 --> 01:00:45.920
 across the blood brain barrier.

01:00:45.920 --> 01:00:48.720
 - They mistakenly identify the myelin as a virus

01:00:48.720 --> 01:00:51.840
 and chew it off, leaving the bare copper wire exposed.

01:00:51.840 --> 01:00:53.480
 - When the insulation is gone,

01:00:53.480 --> 01:00:56.880
 the electrical signals don't just stop, they short circuit.

01:00:56.880 --> 01:00:58.480
 They leak into surrounding tissues

01:00:58.480 --> 01:01:00.200
 or they slow down to a crawl.

01:01:00.200 --> 01:01:02.600
 - This short-circuiting causes the vast array

01:01:02.600 --> 01:01:06.440
 of MS symptoms, profound fatigue, muscle weakness,

01:01:06.440 --> 01:01:10.640
 severe nerve pain, poor coordination and vision problems.

01:01:10.640 --> 01:01:11.760
 - And because the attacks happen

01:01:11.760 --> 01:01:14.200
 in different areas of the brain at different times,

01:01:14.200 --> 01:01:16.240
 the symptoms are wildly unpredictable.

01:01:16.240 --> 01:01:18.440
 - Furthermore, MS is often characterized

01:01:18.440 --> 01:01:20.920
 by a relapsing remitting pattern.

01:01:20.920 --> 01:01:22.120
 The immune system attacks,

01:01:22.120 --> 01:01:25.760
 causing a massive flare-up of symptoms, then it retreats.

01:01:25.760 --> 01:01:28.040
 - The body manages to repair some of the myelin

01:01:28.040 --> 01:01:29.600
 or reroute the signals

01:01:29.600 --> 01:01:32.920
 and the patient recovers some function, remission.

01:01:32.920 --> 01:01:35.000
 - But over years of repeated attacks,

01:01:35.000 --> 01:01:38.640
 the bare axons eventually scar over, sclerosis,

01:01:38.640 --> 01:01:40.360
 and the nerve dies permanently.

01:01:40.360 --> 01:01:42.960
 - Next is myasthenia gravis.

01:01:42.960 --> 01:01:45.880
 Here, the immune system doesn't attack the wire itself,

01:01:45.880 --> 01:01:47.800
 it attacks the connection point.

01:01:47.800 --> 01:01:50.000
 - It attacks the neuromuscular junction,

01:01:50.000 --> 01:01:54.320
 the exact microscopic gap where the nerve talks to the muscle.

01:01:54.320 --> 01:01:55.800
 - For a muscle to contract,

01:01:55.800 --> 01:01:57.920
 the nerve terminal releases a chemical messenger

01:01:57.920 --> 01:01:59.600
 called acetylcholine.

01:01:59.600 --> 01:02:01.720
 - The acetylcholine floats across the gap

01:02:01.720 --> 01:02:04.240
 and binds to specialized receptors on the muscle fiber,

01:02:04.240 --> 01:02:05.960
 triggering the contraction.

01:02:05.960 --> 01:02:07.600
 - In myasthenia gravis,

01:02:07.600 --> 01:02:10.040
 the immune system produces rogue antibodies

01:02:10.040 --> 01:02:12.160
 that physically bind to and block

01:02:12.160 --> 01:02:14.120
 those acetylcholine receptors.

01:02:14.120 --> 01:02:15.720
 - The nerve is shouting the command,

01:02:15.720 --> 01:02:17.640
 releasing plenty of acetylcholine,

01:02:17.640 --> 01:02:19.360
 but the muscle has earplugs in,

01:02:19.360 --> 01:02:20.840
 the signal never gets through.

01:02:20.840 --> 01:02:23.080
 - And because it is a receptor problem,

01:02:23.080 --> 01:02:25.000
 the defining characteristic of this disease

01:02:25.000 --> 01:02:27.320
 is use-dependent fatigue.

01:02:27.320 --> 01:02:29.040
 - The muscles that get used the most

01:02:29.040 --> 01:02:31.080
 constantly run out of available receptors

01:02:31.080 --> 01:02:32.760
 and fatigue the fastest.

01:02:32.760 --> 01:02:34.200
 - Which is why the very first signs

01:02:34.200 --> 01:02:35.680
 are almost always ocular.

01:02:35.680 --> 01:02:37.280
 The tiny muscles controlling the eyes

01:02:37.280 --> 01:02:40.440
 are constantly making micro-adjustments all day long.

01:02:40.440 --> 01:02:43.720
 - So the patient develops deplopia, double vision,

01:02:43.720 --> 01:02:46.160
 and ptosis, a severe drooping of one

01:02:46.160 --> 01:02:48.560
 or both islands as the day progresses.

01:02:48.560 --> 01:02:51.120
 - The weakness then descends to the facial muscles,

01:02:51.120 --> 01:02:54.040
 causing dangerous difficulty swallowing and speaking.

01:02:54.040 --> 01:02:55.320
 - The treatment involves giving

01:02:55.320 --> 01:02:57.760
 anti-colonous to race medications.

01:02:57.760 --> 01:02:59.400
 These drugs inhibit the enzyme

01:02:59.400 --> 01:03:00.880
 that cleans up acetylcholine,

01:03:00.880 --> 01:03:03.120
 essentially flooding the neuromuscular junction

01:03:03.120 --> 01:03:04.640
 with excess chemical,

01:03:04.640 --> 01:03:07.760
 brute forcing the signal through the blocked receptors.

01:03:07.760 --> 01:03:09.000
 - But this creates a dangerous

01:03:09.000 --> 01:03:11.520
 pharmacological tightrope for the patient.

01:03:11.520 --> 01:03:13.400
 - If the patient doesn't take enough medication,

01:03:13.400 --> 01:03:15.880
 they slip into a myasthenic crisis.

01:03:15.880 --> 01:03:17.360
 The weakness becomes so profound

01:03:17.360 --> 01:03:19.640
 that the intercostal muscles of the chest fail

01:03:19.640 --> 01:03:21.520
 and they physically cannot breathe.

01:03:21.520 --> 01:03:23.680
 - But if they take too much medication,

01:03:23.680 --> 01:03:25.960
 they go into a colonergic crisis.

01:03:25.960 --> 01:03:28.560
 And the acronym for this crisis is delightfully gross,

01:03:28.560 --> 01:03:31.360
 but perfectly descriptive of the physiology.

01:03:31.360 --> 01:03:32.880
 Sludge.

01:03:32.880 --> 01:03:35.280
 - Acetylcholine isn't just used for skeletal muscles,

01:03:35.280 --> 01:03:37.000
 it is the primary neurotransmitter

01:03:37.000 --> 01:03:38.760
 for the entire parasympathetic

01:03:38.760 --> 01:03:41.160
 rest and digest nervous system.

01:03:41.160 --> 01:03:43.080
 - If you flood the body with too much of it,

01:03:43.080 --> 01:03:46.440
 the parasympathetic system goes into massive overdrive.

01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:49.840
 Sludge stands for salivation, lacrimation,

01:03:49.840 --> 01:03:51.920
 which is uncontrollable tearing from the eyes,

01:03:51.920 --> 01:03:53.960
 urination, defecation,

01:03:53.960 --> 01:03:57.560
 gastrointestinal cramping and emesis, vomiting.

01:03:57.560 --> 01:03:59.080
 - Everything is wet.

01:03:59.080 --> 01:04:00.560
 Everything is leaking.

01:04:00.560 --> 01:04:02.240
 Everything is contracting.

01:04:02.240 --> 01:04:03.800
 If a patient presents with weakness

01:04:03.800 --> 01:04:06.240
 and is dripping with saliva and tears,

01:04:06.240 --> 01:04:07.960
 you know they took too much medication.

01:04:07.960 --> 01:04:09.560
 - You have to withhold their daily dose

01:04:09.560 --> 01:04:11.920
 and administer an antidote called atropene

01:04:11.920 --> 01:04:13.720
 to block the parasympathetic receptors

01:04:13.720 --> 01:04:15.520
 and dry them out.

01:04:15.520 --> 01:04:19.600
 The third major autoimmune disorder is Guillain-Barre syndrome.

01:04:19.600 --> 01:04:21.520
 This one is uniquely terrifying

01:04:21.520 --> 01:04:24.000
 because of how fast it moves.

01:04:24.000 --> 01:04:25.880
 - It frequently occurs a week or two

01:04:25.880 --> 01:04:29.280
 after a completely mild routine viral infection

01:04:29.280 --> 01:04:31.240
 or sometimes a vaccination.

01:04:31.240 --> 01:04:34.800
 - The theory is molecular mimicry.

01:04:34.800 --> 01:04:36.520
 The immune system successfully fights off

01:04:36.520 --> 01:04:38.360
 the mild stomach bug or the flu.

01:04:38.360 --> 01:04:39.920
 - But the proteins on that virus

01:04:39.920 --> 01:04:41.760
 look structurally very similar

01:04:41.760 --> 01:04:44.520
 to the myelin on the peripheral nervous system,

01:04:44.520 --> 01:04:46.920
 the nerves outside the brain and spinal cord.

01:04:46.920 --> 01:04:48.920
 - The immune system gets confused,

01:04:48.920 --> 01:04:50.520
 fails to stand down,

01:04:50.520 --> 01:04:53.320
 and starts destroying the peripheral myelin.

01:04:53.320 --> 01:04:54.800
 - And the hallmark of Guillain-Barre

01:04:54.800 --> 01:04:56.600
 is the direction of the attack.

01:04:56.600 --> 01:05:00.680
 It causes an acute rapidly ascending weakness.

01:05:00.680 --> 01:05:01.840
 - Ascending.

01:05:01.840 --> 01:05:05.040
 It always starts at the floor and climbs up.

01:05:05.040 --> 01:05:07.080
 The patient notices tingling and weakness

01:05:07.080 --> 01:05:08.800
 in their toes and feet.

01:05:08.800 --> 01:05:11.240
 - A day later, their legs are paralyzed.

01:05:11.240 --> 01:05:14.280
 Then it climbs to the hips, the trunk, the arms.

01:05:14.280 --> 01:05:17.520
 It is a creeping upward paralysis.

01:05:17.520 --> 01:05:19.120
 - The critical nursing goal here

01:05:19.120 --> 01:05:22.120
 is anticipation of respiratory failure.

01:05:22.120 --> 01:05:24.520
 As that paralysis creeps up the chest wall,

01:05:24.520 --> 01:05:26.880
 you have to monitor their vital capacity.

01:05:26.880 --> 01:05:30.400
 How much air they can forcefully exhale obsessively.

01:05:30.400 --> 01:05:32.760
 - You do not wait for them to turn blue.

01:05:32.760 --> 01:05:34.600
 You must intubate them and place them

01:05:34.600 --> 01:05:37.440
 on a mechanical ventilator before the paralysis reaches

01:05:37.440 --> 01:05:40.840
 the diaphragm and they suffocate while wide awake.

01:05:40.840 --> 01:05:42.800
 - The good news is that because peripheral nerves

01:05:42.800 --> 01:05:45.160
 can regenerate myelin, the vast majority

01:05:45.160 --> 01:05:47.880
 of Guillain-Barre patients eventually recover fully,

01:05:47.880 --> 01:05:50.680
 though it takes months of intense physical therapy.

01:05:50.680 --> 01:05:52.760
 - Finally, in the realm of nerve disorders,

01:05:52.760 --> 01:05:54.920
 the notes cover the cranial nerves.

01:05:54.920 --> 01:05:56.600
 The most painful condition mentioned

01:05:56.600 --> 01:06:00.320
 is trigeminal neuralgia, affecting the fifth cranial nerve,

01:06:00.320 --> 01:06:02.320
 which provides all the sensory input

01:06:02.320 --> 01:06:03.920
 from the face to the brain.

01:06:03.920 --> 01:06:07.760
 - The pain of trigeminal neuralgia is legendary in medicine.

01:06:07.760 --> 01:06:09.800
 It is often described as one of the most intense,

01:06:09.800 --> 01:06:12.440
 unbearable pains known to humanity.

01:06:12.440 --> 01:06:15.200
 It is believed to be caused by a blood vessel

01:06:15.200 --> 01:06:17.160
 physically pulsing against the nerve root

01:06:17.160 --> 01:06:18.640
 at the base of the brain,

01:06:18.640 --> 01:06:21.400
 wearing away the myelin insulation.

01:06:21.400 --> 01:06:24.840
 - This causes the nerve to misfire spontaneously.

01:06:24.840 --> 01:06:26.840
 The patient experiences sudden shock-like

01:06:26.840 --> 01:06:28.720
 stabbing bursts of agony,

01:06:28.720 --> 01:06:31.200
 radiating through their jaw, cheek or eye.

01:06:31.200 --> 01:06:33.960
 - And the triggers are so mundane, it's heartbreaking

01:06:33.960 --> 01:06:36.880
 because the nerve is raw and hypersensitive.

01:06:36.880 --> 01:06:40.040
 Ectopic firing can be triggered by washing the face,

01:06:40.040 --> 01:06:42.680
 brushing the teeth, chewing, talking

01:06:42.680 --> 01:06:46.240
 or even just a gentle draft of cool air hitting the cheek.

01:06:46.240 --> 01:06:48.800
 - The nursing care requires immense empathy.

01:06:48.800 --> 01:06:51.560
 You must ensure their environment is free of drafts.

01:06:51.560 --> 01:06:54.880
 You provide room temperature, very soft foods

01:06:54.880 --> 01:06:56.560
 and teach them to chew exclusively

01:06:56.560 --> 01:06:58.600
 on the unaffected side of their mouth.

01:06:58.600 --> 01:07:01.080
 - These patients will often completely stop eating

01:07:01.080 --> 01:07:03.800
 and abandon all basic facial hygiene

01:07:03.800 --> 01:07:07.640
 out of pure paralyzing fear of triggering an attack.

01:07:07.640 --> 01:07:10.320
 - We also have bells palsy, which affects the seventh

01:07:10.320 --> 01:07:13.600
 cranial nerve, the facial nerve that controls motor movement.

01:07:13.600 --> 01:07:17.440
 This causes a rapid unilateral facial paralysis.

01:07:17.440 --> 01:07:20.160
 - One entire side of the face goes completely slack.

01:07:20.160 --> 01:07:22.560
 The eyelid won't close, the cheek droops,

01:07:22.560 --> 01:07:25.240
 the corner of the mouth sags.

01:07:25.240 --> 01:07:27.920
 - Visually, it looks exactly like a stroke,

01:07:27.920 --> 01:07:30.420
 which is utterly terrifying for the patient.

01:07:30.420 --> 01:07:32.880
 But the notes provide a strict warning.

01:07:32.880 --> 01:07:35.720
 Even if it looks like classic bells palsy,

01:07:35.720 --> 01:07:39.360
 any facial paralysis warrants a full stroke workup in the ER.

01:07:39.360 --> 01:07:42.120
 You never assume it's just the nerve.

01:07:42.120 --> 01:07:43.480
 - But once a stroke is ruled out,

01:07:43.480 --> 01:07:46.560
 bells palsy is generally a temporary inflammation,

01:07:46.560 --> 01:07:48.880
 often viral and is very treatable

01:07:48.880 --> 01:07:51.000
 with a short course of oral steroids.

01:07:51.000 --> 01:07:54.400
 - The biggest nursing priority isn't the drooping mouth,

01:07:54.400 --> 01:07:55.840
 it's the eye.

01:07:55.840 --> 01:07:58.000
 Because the eyelid is paralyzed open,

01:07:58.000 --> 01:08:00.480
 they cannot blink to lubricate the cornea.

01:08:00.480 --> 01:08:03.800
 - If left exposed, the cornea will dry out,

01:08:03.800 --> 01:08:06.520
 ulcerate and cause permanent blindness.

01:08:06.520 --> 01:08:09.080
 You have to manually administer lubricating eye drops

01:08:09.080 --> 01:08:12.840
 all day and physically tape the eyelid shut at night.

01:08:12.840 --> 01:08:14.600
 - I don't immune diseases and infections

01:08:14.600 --> 01:08:17.160
 are often sudden and aggressive.

01:08:17.160 --> 01:08:20.040
 But there is another category of neurological threats

01:08:20.040 --> 01:08:22.920
 that move slowly, insidiously,

01:08:22.920 --> 01:08:25.040
 fundamentally altering how a person moves

01:08:25.040 --> 01:08:27.280
 and exists over years.

01:08:27.280 --> 01:08:29.640
 - This brings us to our final exploration,

01:08:29.640 --> 01:08:33.400
 the slow fades, tumors and generative diseases.

01:08:33.400 --> 01:08:36.440
 - Brain tumors, whether they are benign meningiomas

01:08:36.440 --> 01:08:39.120
 or highly malignant glial blastomas,

01:08:39.120 --> 01:08:41.080
 all cause neurological deficits

01:08:41.080 --> 01:08:42.680
 for the exact same fundamental reason

01:08:42.680 --> 01:08:46.600
 we discussed in the beginning, the Munro Kelly doctrine.

01:08:46.600 --> 01:08:50.160
 - A tumor is a solid mass taking up space in a rigid box.

01:08:50.160 --> 01:08:52.960
 It steadily raises intracranial pressure.

01:08:52.960 --> 01:08:56.400
 - So the symptoms look exactly like a slow moving head bleed,

01:08:56.400 --> 01:08:59.200
 morning headaches that get worse when they bear down,

01:08:59.200 --> 01:09:02.160
 unexplained vomiting, visual changes

01:09:02.160 --> 01:09:05.800
 and new onset seizures as the tumor irritates the cortex.

01:09:05.800 --> 01:09:08.800
 - The primary treatment prior to surgery or radiation

01:09:08.800 --> 01:09:12.440
 is entirely supportive, managing the ICP symptoms

01:09:12.440 --> 01:09:14.720
 with steroids to reduce surrounding edema

01:09:14.720 --> 01:09:16.640
 and anti-seizure medications.

01:09:16.640 --> 01:09:18.680
 - One highly specific surgical intervention,

01:09:18.680 --> 01:09:23.160
 the notes highlight is the transphenoidal hypofisectomy.

01:09:23.160 --> 01:09:24.920
 - This is a procedure to remove a tumor

01:09:24.920 --> 01:09:29.120
 on the pituitary gland, which sits in a tiny bony saddle

01:09:29.120 --> 01:09:32.400
 right at the absolute base of the brain behind the eyes.

01:09:32.400 --> 01:09:34.720
 - To get to it, the neurosurgeon doesn't cut

01:09:34.720 --> 01:09:36.200
 through the top of the skull.

01:09:36.200 --> 01:09:37.880
 They go up through the nasal cavity

01:09:37.880 --> 01:09:39.920
 or they make an incision under the upper lip

01:09:39.920 --> 01:09:42.080
 through the gum line and drill straight back

01:09:42.080 --> 01:09:44.800
 through the sphenoid sinus into the base of the skull.

01:09:44.800 --> 01:09:47.080
 - The post-operative rules for this are intense

01:09:47.080 --> 01:09:48.800
 because they just punch the hole

01:09:48.800 --> 01:09:50.800
 through the floor of the cranial vault.

01:09:50.800 --> 01:09:52.880
 The barrier between the unsterile airway

01:09:52.880 --> 01:09:55.720
 and the sterile brain is breached.

01:09:55.720 --> 01:09:57.600
 - The patient cannot do anything

01:09:57.600 --> 01:10:00.040
 that raises pressure in the head or sinuses.

01:10:00.040 --> 01:10:01.920
 Absolutely no coughing, no sneezing,

01:10:01.920 --> 01:10:04.040
 no blowing the nose, no bending over.

01:10:04.040 --> 01:10:05.440
 - If they sneeze forcefully,

01:10:05.440 --> 01:10:09.040
 the pressure can blow out the surgical graft sealing the hole

01:10:09.040 --> 01:10:12.320
 and they will develop a massive cerebral spinal fluid leak

01:10:12.320 --> 01:10:13.880
 directly down their throat.

01:10:13.880 --> 01:10:15.600
 - And if the surgeon went through the gum line

01:10:15.600 --> 01:10:18.280
 under the upper lip, the patient is strictly forbidden

01:10:18.280 --> 01:10:20.280
 from brushing their teeth with a toothbrush

01:10:20.280 --> 01:10:22.200
 for at least 10 days.

01:10:22.200 --> 01:10:23.760
 The bristles will catch the sutures

01:10:23.760 --> 01:10:25.840
 and tear the incision wide open.

01:10:25.840 --> 01:10:28.840
 - If the patient complains of a constant salty taste

01:10:28.840 --> 01:10:29.840
 in the back of their throat

01:10:29.840 --> 01:10:32.520
 or has clear fluid dripping from their nose,

01:10:32.520 --> 01:10:35.480
 the nurse must catch it and test it for glucose.

01:10:35.480 --> 01:10:38.880
 - Standard nasal mucus contains almost zero glucose,

01:10:38.880 --> 01:10:41.920
 but CSF is packed with it to feed the brain.

01:10:41.920 --> 01:10:45.120
 If the nasal drainage tests positive for high glucose,

01:10:45.120 --> 01:10:47.240
 the floor of the skull is leaking.

01:10:47.240 --> 01:10:49.040
 - From the structural masses of tumors,

01:10:49.040 --> 01:10:51.440
 we move to the degenerative diseases.

01:10:51.440 --> 01:10:55.480
 These are the slow, relentless fades of the nervous system.

01:10:55.480 --> 01:10:57.440
 - The notes contrast to the most tragic,

01:10:57.440 --> 01:11:01.200
 Parkinson's disease and ALS, Lou Gehrig's disease.

01:11:01.200 --> 01:11:03.200
 - They are both profoundly debilitating,

01:11:03.200 --> 01:11:04.960
 but their physiological mechanisms

01:11:04.960 --> 01:11:07.360
 are almost perfect opposites.

01:11:07.360 --> 01:11:09.960
 - Let's examine Parkinson's disease first.

01:11:09.960 --> 01:11:11.720
 - Parkinson's is a failure of chemistry

01:11:11.720 --> 01:11:13.600
 in the brain's motor command center.

01:11:13.600 --> 01:11:16.440
 Specifically, the cells in a deep brain structure

01:11:16.440 --> 01:11:19.840
 called the substantia nigra slowly die off.

01:11:19.840 --> 01:11:21.560
 These cells are responsible for producing

01:11:21.560 --> 01:11:23.680
 the neurotransmitter dopamine.

01:11:23.680 --> 01:11:26.400
 - Dopamine gets a lot of presses the reward chemical,

01:11:26.400 --> 01:11:28.640
 but in the extra-paramidal motor system,

01:11:28.640 --> 01:11:32.120
 it acts like the fine-tuning oil in an engine.

01:11:32.120 --> 01:11:33.200
 - Without enough dopamine,

01:11:33.200 --> 01:11:35.880
 the cortex can generate the command to move,

01:11:35.880 --> 01:11:37.920
 but the basal ganglia cannot smooth out

01:11:37.920 --> 01:11:40.080
 the execution of that movement.

01:11:40.080 --> 01:11:43.000
 - The result is the cardinal signs of Parkinson's,

01:11:43.000 --> 01:11:46.040
 arresting pill-rolling tremor in the hands.

01:11:46.040 --> 01:11:48.800
 Severe muscle rigidity, like a cogwheel

01:11:48.800 --> 01:11:50.640
 when you try to bend their arm.

01:11:50.640 --> 01:11:54.640
 Bradykinesia, which means terribly slow hesitant movement,

01:11:54.640 --> 01:11:57.160
 and a shuffling gate with poor balance.

01:11:57.160 --> 01:11:59.400
 - The physical wiring is intact.

01:11:59.400 --> 01:12:01.240
 The command to walk is there,

01:12:01.240 --> 01:12:02.960
 but the chemical oil is missing,

01:12:02.960 --> 01:12:06.480
 so the engine stutters, freezes, and shakes.

01:12:06.480 --> 01:12:09.200
 - The primary pharmacological treatment is elegant,

01:12:09.200 --> 01:12:11.680
 but inherently flawed over time.

01:12:11.680 --> 01:12:14.320
 We replace the missing chemical with a synthetic drug

01:12:14.320 --> 01:12:16.920
 called levodopa, combined with carbodopa

01:12:16.920 --> 01:12:19.320
 to help it cross the blood brain barrier.

01:12:19.320 --> 01:12:21.640
 - It literally gives the surviving brain cells

01:12:21.640 --> 01:12:25.760
 the raw dopamine they need to execute smooth movement.

01:12:25.760 --> 01:12:27.560
 When a patient first starts taking it,

01:12:27.560 --> 01:12:30.240
 the reversal of symptoms can seem miraculous.

01:12:30.240 --> 01:12:32.120
 They go from frozen in the chair

01:12:32.120 --> 01:12:33.640
 to walking across the room.

01:12:33.640 --> 01:12:35.840
 - But there is a dark side to this treatment.

01:12:35.840 --> 01:12:38.200
 The notes emphasize the critical need to monitor

01:12:38.200 --> 01:12:40.640
 for levodopa toxicity.

01:12:40.640 --> 01:12:43.120
 As the years pass, the disease progresses,

01:12:43.120 --> 01:12:45.720
 and more dopamine-producing cells die.

01:12:45.720 --> 01:12:47.800
 - The brain's therapeutic window,

01:12:47.800 --> 01:12:50.680
 the margin between just enough dopamine to move

01:12:50.680 --> 01:12:52.840
 and too much dopamine,

01:12:52.840 --> 01:12:56.720
 gets incredibly dangerously narrow.

01:12:56.720 --> 01:12:58.680
 Furthermore, the remaining dopamine receptors

01:12:58.680 --> 01:13:01.560
 in the brain become hypersensitive.

01:13:01.560 --> 01:13:03.400
 - This creates a cruel irony.

01:13:03.400 --> 01:13:06.400
 The very dose of levodopa that allows the patient to walk

01:13:06.400 --> 01:13:10.600
 suddenly becomes slightly too high for the facial nerves.

01:13:10.600 --> 01:13:12.800
 Though overstimulation causes uncontrolled

01:13:12.800 --> 01:13:16.280
 involuntary writhing movements called dyskinesias.

01:13:16.280 --> 01:13:19.320
 The grimacing, the spasmodic eye-winking,

01:13:19.320 --> 01:13:20.760
 the constant twitching,

01:13:20.760 --> 01:13:23.520
 it isn't the Parkinson's disease itself.

01:13:23.520 --> 01:13:26.100
 It is the toxicity of the synthetic dopamine

01:13:26.100 --> 01:13:28.840
 overstimulating the sensitive receptors.

01:13:28.840 --> 01:13:31.400
 - Parkinson's is a loss of the chemical signal,

01:13:31.400 --> 01:13:33.360
 but the wiring from the brain to the muscles

01:13:33.360 --> 01:13:35.440
 remains completely intact.

01:13:35.440 --> 01:13:38.360
 ALS, emiotrophic lateral sclerosis

01:13:38.360 --> 01:13:40.400
 is the devastating opposite.

01:13:40.400 --> 01:13:44.240
 - In ALS, the chemical signals in the brain are fine,

01:13:44.240 --> 01:13:46.400
 but the motor neurons themselves,

01:13:46.400 --> 01:13:48.960
 the actual physical, upper and lower wires

01:13:48.960 --> 01:13:50.880
 running from the brain down the spinal cord

01:13:50.880 --> 01:13:53.960
 out to the muscles, wither and die.

01:13:53.960 --> 01:13:57.720
 It is a relentless progressive paralysis.

01:13:57.720 --> 01:13:59.440
 - But the true tragedy of ALS

01:13:59.440 --> 01:14:02.520
 is that it is purely a motor neuron disease.

01:14:02.520 --> 01:14:04.200
 It does not affect sensory nerves

01:14:04.200 --> 01:14:06.680
 and it does not affect the cortex.

01:14:06.680 --> 01:14:11.680
 - The patient's mind stays perfectly devastatingly intact.

01:14:11.680 --> 01:14:14.360
 They can feel a fly land on their arm.

01:14:14.360 --> 01:14:16.240
 They can process complex emotions.

01:14:16.240 --> 01:14:18.400
 Their intellect is untouched.

01:14:18.400 --> 01:14:21.000
 - The signals are generated perfectly in the brain,

01:14:21.000 --> 01:14:22.800
 but the wires are dead.

01:14:22.800 --> 01:14:24.960
 The commands never reach the muscles.

01:14:24.960 --> 01:14:28.200
 The muscles weaken, waste away from disuse, atrophy,

01:14:28.200 --> 01:14:30.920
 and twitch uncontrollably in fasciculations

01:14:30.920 --> 01:14:32.280
 as the nerves die.

01:14:32.280 --> 01:14:35.240
 - The patient becomes trapped inside a failing body.

01:14:35.240 --> 01:14:38.000
 Life expectancy is typically just two to three years

01:14:38.000 --> 01:14:39.360
 from diagnosis.

01:14:39.360 --> 01:14:40.800
 - Because the paralysis eventually

01:14:40.800 --> 01:14:42.800
 reaches the intercostal muscles of the chest

01:14:42.800 --> 01:14:45.480
 and the diaphragm, the ultimate cause of death

01:14:45.480 --> 01:14:47.720
 is usually respiratory failure.

01:14:47.720 --> 01:14:49.760
 - This is why Professor Marpol specifically

01:14:49.760 --> 01:14:51.960
 highlights a clinical priority.

01:14:51.960 --> 01:14:55.040
 If an ALS patient is experiencing dyspnea,

01:14:55.040 --> 01:14:57.920
 difficulty breathing, the absolute first intervention

01:14:57.920 --> 01:15:00.800
 is always to administer oxygen.

01:15:00.800 --> 01:15:02.760
 - In a normal patient, if they are short of breath,

01:15:02.760 --> 01:15:04.440
 you sit them up to expand their lungs.

01:15:04.440 --> 01:15:07.800
 But with ALS, elevating the head of the bed isn't enough

01:15:07.800 --> 01:15:10.960
 because the actual physical mechanics of the diaphragm

01:15:10.960 --> 01:15:12.560
 are failing.

01:15:12.560 --> 01:15:14.240
 - They cannot pull the air in.

01:15:14.240 --> 01:15:16.480
 You must supplement the oxygen concentration

01:15:16.480 --> 01:15:19.000
 of whatever shallow breaths they can take.

01:15:19.000 --> 01:15:20.880
 - We have covered an immense amount

01:15:20.880 --> 01:15:22.760
 of physiological ground today.

01:15:22.760 --> 01:15:24.920
 Looking back over Professor Marpol's notes

01:15:24.920 --> 01:15:28.160
 from the massive kinetic trauma of a severed spinal cord

01:15:28.160 --> 01:15:31.520
 causing a sympathetic civil war

01:15:31.520 --> 01:15:34.000
 to the microscopic auto-immune attack

01:15:34.000 --> 01:15:37.200
 stripping the myelin off an axon and MS.

01:15:37.200 --> 01:15:40.560
 - The overarching theme is the sheer mechanical reality

01:15:40.560 --> 01:15:41.880
 of the nervous system.

01:15:42.760 --> 01:15:45.840
 - It is delicate yet incredibly resilient.

01:15:45.840 --> 01:15:47.680
 - We've explored the desperate math

01:15:47.680 --> 01:15:50.040
 of cerebral perfusion pressure,

01:15:50.040 --> 01:15:51.680
 calculating how to keep blood flowing

01:15:51.680 --> 01:15:55.320
 into a rigid skull against rising intracranial swelling.

01:15:55.320 --> 01:15:56.800
 - We've seen the strict physical rules

01:15:56.800 --> 01:15:59.600
 of keeping a spine perfectly straight

01:15:59.600 --> 01:16:02.560
 and the chemical type ropes of treating ischemic strokes

01:16:02.560 --> 01:16:06.560
 with clot busters and Parkinson's with synthetic dopamine.

01:16:06.560 --> 01:16:10.240
 It is the ultimate balancing act of homeostasis.

01:16:10.240 --> 01:16:12.280
 But before we wrap up this deep dive,

01:16:12.280 --> 01:16:14.760
 I wanna look back at one of the very first things mentioned

01:16:14.760 --> 01:16:17.920
 in the notes regarding altered levels of consciousness

01:16:17.920 --> 01:16:19.640
 and pose a final thought.

01:16:19.640 --> 01:16:20.880
 - Yes, please.

01:16:20.880 --> 01:16:22.960
 In those early slides, the professor mentions

01:16:22.960 --> 01:16:26.760
 two specific, terrifying neurological states

01:16:26.760 --> 01:16:28.400
 that challenge everything we assume

01:16:28.400 --> 01:16:30.440
 about what it means to be human.

01:16:30.440 --> 01:16:34.040
 A-kinetic mutism and locked-in syndrome.

01:16:34.040 --> 01:16:38.080
 In A-kinetic mutism, often caused by frontal lobe damage,

01:16:38.080 --> 01:16:40.720
 the patient is awake, their eyes are open,

01:16:40.720 --> 01:16:43.680
 they will physically track you as you walk across the room.

01:16:43.680 --> 01:16:47.000
 - But they are entirely unresponsive to the environment.

01:16:47.000 --> 01:16:50.960
 They lack the will or the internal drive to speak or move.

01:16:50.960 --> 01:16:53.640
 The engine is running, but the driver is gone.

01:16:53.640 --> 01:16:55.840
 - And the other side of that coin is locked-in syndrome.

01:16:55.840 --> 01:16:58.960
 - Exactly, locked-in syndrome, which is often caused

01:16:58.960 --> 01:17:01.320
 by a very specific ischemic stroke

01:17:01.320 --> 01:17:05.120
 in the pawns of the brainstem is the inverse.

01:17:05.120 --> 01:17:07.200
 - The patient is fully conscious.

01:17:07.200 --> 01:17:09.160
 Their cortex is pristine.

01:17:09.160 --> 01:17:12.120
 They are processing every sight, every sound,

01:17:12.120 --> 01:17:15.080
 every conversation happening around their bed perfectly.

01:17:15.080 --> 01:17:18.320
 - But the stroke has severed all descending motor pathways.

01:17:18.320 --> 01:17:19.920
 They are entirely disconnected

01:17:19.920 --> 01:17:22.160
 from every motor output in their body.

01:17:22.160 --> 01:17:24.280
 - They cannot move a single muscle,

01:17:24.280 --> 01:17:27.000
 except usually the ability to blink

01:17:27.000 --> 01:17:28.880
 or move their eyes vertically.

01:17:28.880 --> 01:17:31.320
 They are buried alive inside their own skull.

01:17:31.320 --> 01:17:34.520
 - It challenges our deepest philosophical assumptions.

01:17:34.520 --> 01:17:37.560
 - It's humans, we tend to define ourselves by our actions,

01:17:37.560 --> 01:17:41.160
 our speech, our physical impact on the world around us.

01:17:41.160 --> 01:17:44.560
 We are what we do, but if your brain is perfectly awake,

01:17:44.560 --> 01:17:47.040
 containing a vibrant, infinite universe of thought,

01:17:47.040 --> 01:17:50.160
 emotion and memory, yet you are entirely silent

01:17:50.160 --> 01:17:52.360
 to the outside world, unable to affect

01:17:52.360 --> 01:17:56.120
 a single change upon it, where does the self exist?

01:17:56.120 --> 01:17:58.240
 - It leaves you to wonder about the profound,

01:17:58.240 --> 01:18:01.160
 silent inner worlds of those whose neurological pathways

01:18:01.160 --> 01:18:04.440
 have gone dark, and the absolute sanctity of the organ

01:18:04.440 --> 01:18:06.000
 that generates that world.

01:18:06.000 --> 01:18:09.040
 - That is heavy and absolutely fascinating.

01:18:09.040 --> 01:18:11.480
 We think we are our bodies, but we are really

01:18:11.480 --> 01:18:13.320
 just the electricity inside them,

01:18:13.320 --> 01:18:16.440
 experiencing the world through a delicate web of wires.

01:18:16.440 --> 01:18:18.600
 We started this deep dive talking about broken bones

01:18:18.600 --> 01:18:21.640
 and x-rays, about the comfort of a clear, jagged white line

01:18:21.640 --> 01:18:23.560
 and a simple diagnosis.

01:18:23.560 --> 01:18:25.240
 - But we've seen today that the nervous system

01:18:25.240 --> 01:18:26.600
 is not binary.

01:18:26.600 --> 01:18:29.120
 It is a constantly shifting sea of pressures,

01:18:29.120 --> 01:18:32.280
 a desperate dance of chemistry, physics and electricity,

01:18:32.280 --> 01:18:35.320
 hiding inside an impenetrable bone box.

01:18:35.320 --> 01:18:37.440
 - The diagnostic waters are murky,

01:18:37.440 --> 01:18:39.720
 and the stakes are life and death.

01:18:39.720 --> 01:18:42.760
 But hopefully after today, the mechanics of how we survive

01:18:42.760 --> 01:18:44.800
 are a little easier to navigate.

01:18:44.800 --> 01:18:46.840
 Thank you for joining us on this deep dive.

01:18:46.840 --> 01:18:49.400
 Keep learning, keep the synapses firing,

01:18:49.400 --> 01:18:51.000
 and we will see you next time.

